 ],
 "literal": "Carpenter made small toy-horse cart / even though they do not get pleasure by riding, with hands / like children who experience pleasure by dragging, / even though we do not experience pleasure by embracing, of the good chariot / chief of the pond-filled town, friendship / having made, I rejoiced; my heart is glad, the bangles became tight.",
 "translation": "Like children who, though they cannot ride and enjoy the small toy-horse cart made by a carpenter, find joy in dragging it with their hands, even so, though we do not get the pleasure of embracing him, the friendship of the lord of the pond-filled town with his fine chariot has made us rejoice, and our bangles have become tight on our arms.",
 "modern": "தச்சன் செய்த சிறிய குதிரை வண்டியை ஏறி ஓட்டி இன்புறாவிட்டாலும், கைகளால் இழுத்து இன்புறும் சிறு பிள்ளைகளைப் போல, நல்ல தேரையுடைய பொய்கைகள் நிறைந்த ஊரின் தலைவனுடைய நட்பை, நாம் தழுவி இன்புறாவிட்டாலும், அவனோடு நட்புச் செய்ததாலேயே நான் இன்புற்றேன்; என் வளையல்களும் கைகளில் செறிந்து நின்றன.",
 "explanation": "The confidante (tōḻi) speaks to the heroine or the hero's messenger about the nature of the hero's friendship. In this Marutham poem, she compares their relationship with the hero to children playing with a toy cart. Just as children who cannot ride a toy cart still find joy in simply dragging it around, the heroine finds joy in the mere existence of the hero's friendship, even when they cannot be physically united. This friendship is so nourishing that her body flourishes, and her bangles, which had slipped off in her grief of separation, now fit tightly on her arms again. The imagery of the toy cart beautifully illustrates that love brings joy in all its forms, even in simple association.",
